ero  well Yale doesn't offer a major in that  particular field of study but alex has  still found role models in his life who  have taught him that not all superheroes  need a costume or a cape  Alex learned the unconventional way to  reach his goals a path that has taken  him to study as an eli whitney's student  in Yale College he is a certified  Department of Defense interrogator a  strategic debriefer and a military  source handler but not content with  merely fighting for freedom abroad  alex has also served at home to ensure  justice on the streets of the New York  of New York City as a part of the New  York City's Police Department then there  are students like Sara Barbra who will  also hear about from today she's a  student was nominated to speak by her  peers of the Yale student veterans  Council and Ananas Lee approved by the  Planning Committee for this event  today she will ask all of us to take  time to celebrate and to honor veterans  in our community during her time  commissioned with the Army Medical  Service Corps Sarah constantly felt  those two emotions as she cherished  being welcomed into a new family that of  the army family and she aimed to protect  those around her by training her fellow  colleagues  it is that generous and reflective  attitude that has carried her from her  first Veterans Day speech eight years  ago at her undergraduate alma mater the  University of Dayton across the ocean to  Iraq before coming here to Connecticut  to be with us today wherever she is  stationed Sara added more relations to  her ever-growing family and we are  privileged to be among them and then we  are also lucky because there are  students like Joel Johnson who's with us  today they dedicate themselves to their  country their studies and their families  a father of four Joel is pursuing a  master's degree in global affairs  through the Jackson Institute he has led  soldiers at the platoon and company  level through two deployments to Iraq  Joel currently is has just begun  training as a US farming US Army foreign  op area officer with Southeast Asia as  his regional focus these students are  but three voices in the larger veterans  community throughout campus veterans  play an instrumental role in our  day-to-day life whether they manage our  facilities plant they assist us in  setting up computers they teach courses  protect us as members of the police  department or administer programs and  services veterans serve and influence  the university at all levels few  ambassadors of the veterans community  reflect all that diversity more than  Reverend Harry Adams he has an alum of  the college in the Divinity School a  former dean of the Divinity School  chaplain of the University and a member  of the Yale bands most importantly he  could single-handedly perform all parts  of the ceremony today but he's an alum  who continues to incorporate the Yale  committee into his life when when he has  opportunities to interact with our  current veterans on campus fellowship at  Yale does not remain constrained within  borders it branches across all the  schools and all the departments at Yale  it intrudes traditional limitations of  hierarchy and it creates conversation  between faculty staff and students alike  it's George W Pearson famously described  the university yell is at once a  tradition a company of scholars a  society of friends our veterans  community resonates within that  University tradition of fellowship in  the past years we've seen the individual  veteran societies emerge in our  professional schools and our college  graduate schools all of these groups  assist student veterans in leveraging  their formal experiences to cultivate  camaraderie and Federer turnit II many  students participate in the Yale student  veterans Council which is one of our is  worked with us on the programming and  they incorporate the Yale tradition into  their goals by having meetings every  month at Morrie's the faculty and staff  joined the dialog through the veterans  VL Veterans Association which is also  one of our sponsors  it's all of these communities that make  me optimistic about continuing to make  yells culture and policies more veteran  friendly so let's continue to justify  that optimism let's continue to make  sure that all these conversations about  the future of our Republic today and  every day so that the ideals that  embodied by the service that have been  given by our students by our faculty and  staff and former generations include the  service of future generations and ours  it is now my distinct pleasure at this  time to call upon the officers from the  Yale University Police Department who  serve as our honor guard please stand as  they offer the presentation of colors  followed by the playing of our national  anthem and remain standing for  invocation  please bow your head with some heads  with me as I pray Heavenly Father we  humbly bow before you on this special  occasion as we honor the many service  men and women who have served the United  States and given their lives in its  defence we acknowledge your mighty hand  of protection over this country and its  brave men and women we thank you Lord  for the generations of men and women who  have fought to preserve our freedom  citizens who have been willing to face  terrible combat agonizing loneliness and  austere conditions in order to preserve  Liberty for their fellow Americans Lord  we thank you for providing America with  men and women of the highest character  once you never quit fighting for what is  right  even if it cost them their lives god we  humbly ask for your providence and grace  to remain over this country and that you  would continue to raise up courageous  Americans who will willingly give up  everything in order to protect the  freedoms which you have so graciously  granted to us we acknowledge your mighty  hand on this nation and its leaders and  we ask you to continue to guide us Lord  Heavenly Father we pray for your hedge  of protection around our soldiers  sailors airmen and Marines serving at  home and abroad lord provide our  servicemen and women with hope and dark  times peace after the fighting has  subsided and your favor upon them always  we ask for your perfect wisdom to guide  those in positions of authority within  the military so that they will lead  courageously and live as an example to  those who serve under them Lord for  those veterans who still deal with  painful reminders of their service we  ask that you would comfort them and  bring restoration we ask this for the  ones who have been crippled or wounded  in the service of this country as well  as for those who struggle to find peace  long after the battle has subsided for  veterans who still deal with  post-traumatic stress struggling to  sleep peacefully because of painful  memories we asked for you to intervene  and begin to heal these invisible scars  finally we remember those who have lost  sons or daughters husbands or wives  fathers or mothers and we asked  that you would send them your peace that  passes all understanding we humbly pray  these things in your mighty name Lord  God we thank you Heavenly Father for the  opportunity to come together to honor  those who have served this country those  who continue to serve and the ones who  have paid the highest price for the  liberties we still enjoy we ask that you  would bless this Veterans Day service  and all of us here today in Jesus name  we pray amen  please be seated  good afternoon ladies and gentlemen our  distinguished guests Provost [ __ ] and  mayor elect ARP our senior military  officers representing all branches of  the armed forces and all veterans both  present here and elsewhere thank you for  joining us today we are here today to  celebrate and to honor these are the  critical aspects of today's tribute  Veterans Day and contrast Memorial Day  it's not a solemn day of remembrance but  a celebration a day to honor 