ng down  on top of him fellow soldiers witnessed  this and they expose themselves to enemy  fire to rush forward and dig through the  rubble with their bare hands  hoping to save his life luckily for me  they did and when he awoke a short while  later he was in a hospital in Japan he  found that his teeth and his skull had  been crushed by the weight of the debris  and his jaw was wired shut well the army  wanted to give a medal they wanted to  give him a Purple Heart but he declined  it immediately  because he thought that if you were to  accept it my grandmother might know that  he'd been injured he told me that in his  darkest moments he thought only of her  well my grandfather would have many dark  moments ahead because after two months  of eating through a straw the army sent  him back to the frontlines to finish his  tour my grandfather he didn't have any  superpowers he couldn't fly didn't wear  a cape he didn't hide behind a secret  identity but what he had was this  incredible public spirit he had the  ability to look beyond his own interests  and put the interests of his community  and his nation before his own as it  turns out this union disability is not  unique to my grandfather but it's been a  hallmark of military servicemembers for  centuries since our very beginning  American men and women have stood up to  fight for freedom and for justice some  like my grandpa did this in foreign  lands and for people they had never met  and others stood up to fight for  freedoms that they themselves couldn't  enjoy at home in 1776 there was Margaret  Corbin in the Battle of Fort Washington  she watched her husband die Manning an  artillery piece and then she stood up  and took over his fighting position and  rain fire down on the British gravely  wounded she became the first woman to  earn a full military pension it would be  over a hundred years before she would  learn the right to vote and then a  hundred years before the passing of the  Civil Rights Act that was Sergeant  William Carney sergeant Carney inspired  the 54th Massachusetts volunteers when  he picked up the Union colors and  charged into oncoming fire he had been  born a slave in Norfolk Virginia and  that act was the first act to iron an  African American the Congressional Medal  of Honor  and then there's my friend don't Tony  Greco  well Tony in 2009 was humiliated by our  armed forces when he was discharged  under Don't Ask Don't Tell yet Tony Toni  harbored no resentment and in 2011  one week after Don't Ask Don't Tell was  repealed Jiri enlisted and today as his  home state of Pennsylvania continues to  deny his right to marry Tony prepares to  go on a deployment to Afghanistan one of  the final of our nation Tony has chosen  service over self and these these  stories these veterans their courage  selflessness and love they inspire us  all and that's what superheroes do in  society's darkest days when sinister  forces seemed to be conspiring against  us threaten to take over the world  superheroes remind us that all is not  lost they remind us that while we might  not have superpowers ourselves when we  choose to overlook our own concerns and  stand together we can accomplish great  things  superheroes inspire hope so today I want  to say thank you to all the superheroes  that I never read about in comic books  to my grandpa to tony greco and to all  the men and women who have ever served  thank you  let us pray Almighty God as we remember  those who have served their country with  commitment and with curry we give thanks  for their dedication and sacrifice and  we pray your continued blessing upon our  nation and upon all the nations of our  world as we go from this place of  honored memory we beseech you to bless  us that we may strive with new  dedication for peace in our world and  for justice in our common life go in  peace amen  the colors are about to be retired and  as we conclude our Veterans Day program  you would like to thank the planning  committee for developing our program all  those who participate in the program as  well as Marge Berman who coordinated our  events today as we retire the program of  the colors we ask you to remain standing  and then we will be dismissed thank you  for joining us
 

